course: dictionary
lesson: 2285
title: Cress and the clues around it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cress is a small plant with tiny green leaves. Its leaves taste peppery, so people put them in salads and sandwiches. You may not know this word yet, but the sentence helps: «a cress plant on the windowsill leans towards the light» tells you it is a small plant you can grow in a window.
explain: Look at the word watercress. It joins two words, water and cress, so it is a kind of cress that grows in water. When you meet a long word, check whether you know the small words inside it.
explain: Cress is uncountable, like water, sand or rice. We say some cress, not two cresses. Check your meaning by putting it in the sentence: «I put some cress on my sandwich» still makes sense, so you have understood the word.
fact: Cress is a small plant with tiny green leaves that taste peppery.
fact: Cress is often eaten in salads and sandwiches.
fact: Watercress is a kind of cress that grows in water.
fact: In science lessons, children grow cress on damp cotton wool or kitchen roll to test what seeds need: water, warmth and light.
fact: Plants move towards light: «a cress plant on the windowsill leans towards the light.»
exercise: «A cress plant on the windowsill leans towards the light.» What is a cress plant? | A large tree that grows in a forest | A kind of bread roll | *A small plant with tiny green leaves that taste peppery | A small box for keeping pencils
exercise: Find and copy one word that is a kind of cress, a plant that grows in water. | sandwich | *watercress | windowsill | light
exercise: Ella says cress tastes sweet, like sugar. Is she right? | *No, cress tastes peppery | It tastes like chocolate | Yes, it tastes like sugar
test: Which two things are in an egg and cress sandwich? | Cheese and pickle | Ham and tomato | Jam and butter | *Egg and cress
test: In science lessons, why do children grow cress on damp cotton wool? | To make the cotton wool dry | To keep the room smelling nice | To make a salad for lunch only | *To see what seeds need, such as water, warmth and light
exam: Which sentence uses cress correctly, as an uncountable noun? | I put three cresses on my sandwich | I put two cresses on my sandwich | I put a cress on my sandwich | *I put some cress on my sandwich
